{
  "concept": "In a solid, the energy separation between the filled valence band and the empty conduction band — the band gap — determines whether electrons can be promoted into mobile states, classifying the material as a conductor, semiconductor, or insulator.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Three side-by-side band diagrams sharing an implicit vertical energy axis (energy increases upward), with a faint 'Energy →' arrow and label along the left edge",
    "Panel 1 heading: 'Insulator' (bold, centered above its blocks)",
    "Panel 2 heading: 'Semiconductor' (bold, centered above its blocks)",
    "Panel 3 heading: 'Conductor' (bold, centered above its blocks)",
    "Blue-violet filled rectangle labeled 'Valence' at the bottom of each panel (identical width and height in all three)",
    "Green filled rectangle labeled 'Conduction' above the valence block in each panel (identical width and height in all three)",
    "White empty gap region between the two blocks in each panel, its height the only quantity that differs across panels",
    "Vertical dashed lines marking the left and right edges of each white gap region (omitted or vestigial in the conductor panel where the gap is a thin sliver)",
    "Double-headed vertical arrow spanning each gap, labeled 'Band gap'; in the conductor panel the label sits outside the diagram to the right with a short leader line",
    "Numeric gap value annotation (in eV) beside each panel's gap arrow",
    "Interactive fourth 'adjustable' panel (or highlighted active panel) whose conduction band moves vertically with the slider, with a live classification caption reading 'Conductor / Semiconductor / Insulator'",
    "Small filled circles representing electrons sitting at the top of each valence band",
    "Promotion arrow: an upward arrow from a valence electron toward the conduction band, drawn as a solid arrow that reaches the conduction band when promotion succeeds and a dashed, truncated arrow that stalls in the gap when it fails",
    "Thermal-energy marker: a horizontal dotted line (and 'available energy, ~kT / thermal excitation' label) drawn a fixed height above the valence band top, compared against each gap height",
    "Result callout text near the clicked panel: 'Electron promoted — current flows' or 'Not enough energy — no conduction'",
    "Legend mapping green = conduction band (empty), blue-violet = valence band (filled), white = forbidden gap"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "bandGap",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Band gap (eV)",
      "range": [0, 6, 0.05],
      "default": 3.0,
      "teaches": "The conduction band slides up or down relative to the fixed valence band, and the material's name changes with it — conductor, semiconductor, insulator are not three different pictures but one picture with one varying distance."
    },
    {
      "id": "promoteElectron",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking a panel launches an electron upward with a fixed packet of available energy; it reaches the conduction band only when the gap is smaller than that energy, so a large gap physically blocks conduction."
    },
    {
      "id": "thermalEnergy",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Available thermal energy",
      "options": ["Room temperature (300 K)", "Heated (600 K)"],
      "default": "Room temperature (300 K)",
      "teaches": "Raising the temperature raises the energy marker, so a semiconductor that could not conduct now can — conductivity of semiconductors increases with temperature, while an insulator's gap is still far too wide."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "One diagram, three materials",
      "narration": "All three panels use the same filled valence band and the same empty conduction band; only the white gap between them changes height. That single distance is the whole classification.",
      "state": { "bandGap": 3.0, "thermalEnergy": "Room temperature (300 K)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Widen the gap: an insulator",
      "narration": "Drag the conduction band far above the valence band. With a gap of several electron-volts, the label reads Insulator — electrons have nowhere accessible to go.",
      "state": { "bandGap": 5.5, "thermalEnergy": "Room temperature (300 K)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Try to promote an electron",
      "narration": "Click the panel. The promotion arrow rises only as far as the available-energy line and stalls inside the forbidden gap: no electron reaches the conduction band, so no current flows.",
      "state": { "bandGap": 5.5, "thermalEnergy": "Room temperature (300 K)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Shrink to a semiconductor",
      "narration": "Bring the conduction band down to about 1 eV above the valence band. The label switches to Semiconductor: the gap is now comparable to the energy a few electrons can pick up.",
      "state": { "bandGap": 1.0, "thermalEnergy": "Room temperature (300 K)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Heat the semiconductor",
      "narration": "Raise the thermal energy marker. The same 1 eV gap that mostly blocked promotion at room temperature is now crossed — semiconductor conductivity rises with temperature.",
      "state": { "bandGap": 1.0, "thermalEnergy": "Heated (600 K)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Close the gap: a conductor",
      "narration": "Slide the bands almost together. The white region becomes a sliver, electrons flow into empty conduction states with essentially no energy cost, and the material is a conductor.",
      "state": { "bandGap": 0.05, "thermalEnergy": "Room temperature (300 K)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Conductors, semiconductors, and insulators are not different kinds of diagrams — they are the same diagram with one distance changed: how much energy an electron must gain to leave the filled valence band and enter the empty conduction band.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three vertically stacked block pairs arranged left to right on a white background, all sharing identical block widths and colors: a muted blue-violet rectangle labeled 'Valence' on the bottom and a yellow-green rectangle labeled 'Conduction' above it, both with black centered sans-serif labels. Energy increases upward; the three panels are staggered so each valence block sits progressively lower/higher as in the original, emphasizing that only the white gap height differs. Left panel (Insulator) has the tallest white gap with tall vertical dashed lines on both edges and a long black double-headed arrow labeled 'Band gap' in its middle; middle panel (Semiconductor) repeats this with roughly half the gap height; right panel (Conductor) has only a thin white sliver, no room for an internal arrow, so 'Band gap' is written outside to the right. Bold black headings sit above each panel. Add small dark electron dots along the top edge of each valence band, a dotted horizontal 'available thermal energy' line drawn at a fixed height above the valence band top, and an animated promotion arrow that is solid green when it reaches the conduction band and dashed red-grey when it stalls inside the gap. Show the live gap value in eV next to each arrow and a bold classification caption under the adjustable panel that reads Conductor (<0.1 eV), Semiconductor (~0.1–3 eV), or Insulator (>3 eV)."
}

