  "keyInsight": "Insulators, semiconductors and conductors are not three different diagrams — they are the same diagram with one distance changed: how much energy an electron must gain to leave the filled valence band and reach the empty conduction band.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Layout: three block-pairs left to right (Insulator, Semiconductor, Conductor) on white, sized so the drawing fills the container edge to edge with only small margins — tighten the viewBox so no dead space remains below the diagram. All body text at least 12 px effective size; 'Conduction', 'Valence' and 'Band gap' should be clearly larger than the surrounding chrome and centered on their blocks. Colors: muted blue-violet 'Valence' rectangle at bottom, yellow-green 'Conduction' rectangle above, white forbidden gap between, thin dark outlines. Insulator has the tallest white gap with dashed edge guides and a long black double-headed 'Band gap' arrow; semiconductor repeats this at roughly half the height; conductor shows only a thin sliver with 'Band gap' written outside to the LEFT on a short leader line, well separated from any dotted energy-marker text on the right. STEP-0 DEFAULT MUST MATCH THE TEXTBOOK: no energy axis, no eV numbers, no legend, no electron dots, no excitation-energy line, no classification caption — these appear only after a click, a toggle change, or a nonzero slider value. When revealed, the horizontal marker is a neutral grey/blue dotted line labeled 'typical excitation energy (schematic)' with no asserted numeric value at a stated temperature; avoid any claim like '0.70 eV at 300 K'. Promotion arrow is solid green when it reaches the conduction band, dashed grey when it stalls in the gap, with a short one-line caption beside it. When the slider is engaged, add a faint upward 'Energy' arrow at the far left, eV values beside each gap arrow, and a bold caption under the adjustable panel: Conductor (<0.1 eV), Semiconductor (~0.1–3 eV), Insulator (>3 eV). Legend, if shown, sits compactly at the bottom with no trailing whitespace."
}

